---
title: Analyze task distribution with the Public API
description: Compare task assignments and agent utilization in Nx Cloud before changes to assignment rules, resources, or parallelism.
filter: 'type:Guides'
topics:
  - 'Nx Cloud'
  - 'Continuous integration'
---

Use the [Nx Cloud Public API](/docs/reference/nx-cloud/public-api) to compare task placement, duration,
and agent utilization for completed distributed CI runs.
The API returns recorded evidence.
It doesn't expose the current assignment-rule configuration or change task distribution.

## Select comparable executions

Choose a small set of completed pipelines for the same CI environment.
Keep the branch, workload, launch templates, and cache conditions comparable.
Compare a baseline period with a period after one configuration change.

Start from a CI pipeline execution (CIPE) or run group and follow its run links.
Select distributed task execution (DTE) runs using the recorded `isDTE` field.
Follow each run's `links.tasks` and fetch all pages needed for the comparison.

Collect the run-group agents and, for an Nx-managed workflow, its steps and instances.
Fetch detail responses only for the agents or instances relevant to the bottleneck.
Respect the organization's shared quota and the response's `Retry-After` header.

## Build a task timeline

For each task, record its ID, `batchId` when present, run ID, hash, status, cache status, agent name, and
start and completion timestamps.
Add prior attempts as separate events when you measure retry work.

Group tasks by agent.
Align task intervals with workflow instance intervals and agent resource samples.
Keep setup, task execution, and cleanup separate.
A workflow instance is one step on one agent, not one task.

Treat missing timestamps as unknown.
Several batched tasks can share the batch result's completion time.
Do not treat those timestamps as exact task boundaries.

A repeated task ID doesn't prove duplicate or wasted work.
Compare the run, hash, configuration, cache status, and attempt history.
Inspect the matching target and task dependencies in the repository before you classify repeated
non-cacheable work.

## Compare resource demand with allocation

Download each relevant agent's `resource-utilization` asset.
Follow [Read raw resource-utilization reports](/docs/kb/read-resource-utilization-reports) to interpret
the records and map central processing unit (CPU) and memory samples to tasks.
Use instance `resourceUsage` or the launch-template allocation to compare demand with capacity.

Agent `cpuCores` and `totalMemory` describe detected system capacity, which can depend on the Nx version
and operating-system limits.
They don't establish the requested resource class.
Agent averages and maxima describe the tracked Nx process set, not all host activity.
CPU uses 100 percent per core.

Use the evidence to distinguish these cases:

| Observation                                  | Next check                                                                               |
| -------------------------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| One agent remains busy after others finish   | Check task placement, dependencies, and the long task's resource demand.                 |
| Agents wait while a long task runs elsewhere | Check the task graph before you add agents.                                              |
| CPU or memory approaches the allocated limit | Compare a larger launch template or lower contention.                                    |
| Setup consumes most of the workflow          | Inspect instance logs and improve setup before task allocation.                          |
| The same work appears on several agents      | Verify task identity, attempts, and dependency consumption before you call it redundant. |

Low average CPU alone doesn't prove that more parallelism will help.
A task can wait on input/output (I/O), dependencies, or an external service.

## Use daily metrics for trends

Use `/task-stats` for daily project-target counts, cache ratios, duration averages, and optional
cache-miss percentiles.
Filter by project, target, CI classification, and a settled date range.
A `target` filter requires `projectName`.

Each row describes one day in Coordinated Universal Time (UTC).
Weight averages by their relevant counts.
Do not average daily percentiles to produce a period percentile.
Use raw task attempts when you need total compute time, exact timing, or separation by CI environment.

Report pipeline wall time and task compute time separately.
Parallel tasks can consume more total compute than the pipeline's elapsed time.
Cache hits and retries can also change the workload between periods.

## Propose one measurable experiment

Inspect the repository's [assignment rules](/docs/kb/assignment-rules),
[CI configuration](/docs/reference/nx-cloud/ci-config), and task graph before a change.

On Nx Agents,
[_dynamic task packing_](/docs/features/ci-features/distribute-task-execution#how-dynamic-task-packing-works)
selects concurrent work from task resource demand.
Assignment rules control eligible agent types.
They aren't the default mechanism for a fixed task count per agent.
Bring-your-own-compute (BYOC) configurations can use different parallelism controls.

Choose one experiment supported by the evidence, such as a different eligible agent type or a smaller
setup step.
Before the change, define the success measure as lower pipeline wall time, fewer retries, or lower
compute for a comparable workload.

If an artificial intelligence (AI) agent proposes a configuration change, require it to show the
current configuration, the supporting API evidence, and a validation plan.
Review the change before rollout.
Repeat the same comparison after rollout and retain a rollback option.
